{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#398","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#398","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#398\",\"parent_revision\":\"b883796ddec5fa619d316ac6604f4bdac23d34b8\",\"root_revision\":\"7237f60b7afc59e85a45301da8f316c273bf405105d9e1590d1c7a7e8abb4ed3\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nProcess audit of the full backlog item lifecycle (create \u2192 groom \u2192 discuss \u2192 research \u2192 validate \u2192 feasibility \u2192 plan \u2192 implement \u2192 close/resolve) using the /improve-processes excellence checklist and triage protocol. 10 findings identified across 3 severity levels. 2 HIGH: (F1) no feasibility gate between RT-ICA APPROVED and SAM planning, (F9) 6 implied handoffs that break autonomous execution. 5 MEDIUM: (F2) no discussion/interview step, (F4) vague 'is job valid' condition, (F5) non-sequential step numbering, (F6) no groomer output validation, (F10) draft lifecycle doc not promoted. 3 LOW: (F3) RT-ICA staleness, (F7) auto-mode P1 default, (F8) fact-check auto-push. Full report: .claude/docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-02T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-02T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **backlog lifecycle process gaps \u2014 10 findings from improve-processes audit** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 /improve-processes audit\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-02\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-03)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:16:48.324473Z</sub>\\n\\n9/10 \u2014 High-leverage process-control work. Affects all backlog throughput. The two HIGH findings (feasibility gate, implied handoffs) directly block reliable autonomous lifecycle execution. Status:verified signals work is complete; priority remains for retrospective reference.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:16:52.048529Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: Reliable autonomous create\u2192groom\u2192work\u2192plan\u2192implement\u2192close/resolve flow when implied handoffs require manual operator memory\\n- Bottlenecks: Ungated RT-ICA\u2192planning transition (F1); 6 implied cross-skill handoffs (F9 Gaps A\u2013F)\\n- Risk if unchanged: Wasted planning cycles on infeasible items; stalled auto mode at each implied handoff; inconsistent lifecycle execution\\n- Downstream: Every future backlog item goes through this lifecycle \u2014 process gaps compound across all items\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:16:55.547012Z</sub>\\n\\n- Reliable autonomous end-to-end backlog item lifecycle execution in auto mode\\n- Eliminated waste from ungated SAM planning on infeasible items (F1)\\n- Explicit machine-followable handoffs at all 6 previously-implied transitions (F9)\\n- Deterministic groomer output via validation before section writes (F6)\\n- Canonical lifecycle documentation promoted from draft (F10)\\n- Normalized skill navigation (F5) reduces agent cognitive load\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:16:59.204763Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen the backlog lifecycle runs correctly:\\n\\n1. A feasibility decision gate exists between RT-ICA APPROVED and SAM planning invocation \u2014 the agent or orchestrator can evaluate go/no-go without running the full SAM pipeline.\\n2. All 6 implied handoffs (A: create\u2192groom, B: groom\u2192milestone, C: milestone\u2192start, D: complete-implementation\u2192resolve, E: resolve\u2192complete-milestone, F: fact-check\u2192groom) are represented as explicit next-step contracts or a canonical lifecycle state machine.\\n3. Groomed output from the groomer agent passes a section-presence and scope-boundary validation check before being written to the item file.\\n4. The lifecycle canonical document (`backlog-item-lifecycle.md`) is the single source of truth for phase transitions, node contracts, and handoff protocols \u2014 referenced by all relevant skills.\\n5. Decision conditions (e.g., 'is job valid?') use observable checks with named signals, not prose heuristics.\\n6. Step numbering/phase model in skills is deterministic and navigable by agents without backtracking.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:17:02.786551Z</sub>\\n\\nThe target state observable once this work is done:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` is canonical, fully verified, and referenced by all lifecycle skills\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.draft.md` is either promoted (content merged into canonical doc) or superseded with a redirect note\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` (state machine) reflects current skill behaviors including any new gates added\\n- Each lifecycle skill (`create-backlog-item`, `groom-backlog-item`, `work-backlog-item`) references the canonical lifecycle doc in its opening section\\n- Grooming swarm includes a post-groomer validation pass before `backlog_groom` MCP calls\\n- `work-backlog-item` step structure uses named phases (Create, Groom, Work) with deterministic entry conditions \u2014 no decimal sub-steps or lettered variants\\n- All 6 implied handoffs have either: (a) explicit `next-step` output contract in the producing skill, or (b) a canonical lifecycle state machine entry that any consumer skill can reference\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:17:06.259015Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given the full lifecycle runs in auto mode from `/dh:create-backlog-item` through `/dh:work-backlog-item resolve`, the orchestrator does not stall at any of the 6 previously-implied handoffs (A\u2013F from Finding 9) \u2014 each has an explicit next-step signal or state machine reference.\\n2. Given an item that passes RT-ICA APPROVED, there exists an observable feasibility decision point before `/dh:add-new-feature` is invoked \u2014 the orchestrator can output go/no-go/defer without running the full 4\u20136 agent SAM pipeline.\\n3. Given a groomer agent produces output missing required sections (Acceptance Criteria, Priority, or Dependencies), the validation step catches the gap and either re-prompts or escalates before writing to the item file.\\n4. Given `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md`, it is reachable via a markdown link from each of the lifecycle skills (`create-backlog-item`, `groom-backlog-item`, `work-backlog-item`).\\n5. Given the `work-backlog-item` skill, its step structure uses named phases (Create / Groom / Work) with deterministic preconditions \u2014 no non-sequential decimal sub-steps or lettered variants that require an agent to hold multiple numbering schemes simultaneously.\\n6. Given `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.draft.md`, it is either: (a) promoted and merged into the canonical lifecycle doc, or (b) marked superseded with a redirect to the canonical file.\\n7. Given a Low-Medium severity finding, there is a documented policy for how it is counted in severity totals \u2014 eliminating the F3 reporting ambiguity.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-05T08:17:09.677237Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` \u2014 source audit with Findings 1-10 and severities |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.draft.md` \u2014 DRAFT lifecycle doc (2026-02-27, [VERIFY] markers, old skill paths) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` \u2014